WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.346
Identity federation cannot replace every
application credential.

2
00:00:03.346 --> 00:00:06.857
Third-party APIs may require keys, older
databases may require

3
00:00:06.857 --> 00:00:10.557
passwords, and webhook protocols may
require shared signing material.

4
00:00:10.557 --> 00:00:12.834
Certificates and private keys still need

5
00:00:12.834 --> 00:00:15.112
controlled access, renewal, and rotation.

6
00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:19.002
For signing operations, a managed
cryptographic service may keep the

7
00:00:19.002 --> 00:00:23.366
private key outside application memory
entirely when the protocol permits it.

8
00:00:23.366 --> 00:00:26.877
For secrets that must be retrieved, use a
managed

9
00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:30.577
store with encryption at rest, secure
transport, authenticated access,

10
00:00:30.577 --> 00:00:34.372
narrowly scoped authorization, auditing,
and controlled versioning and rotation.

11
00:00:34.372 --> 00:00:37.409
Separate permission to read a required
secret from

12
00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:40.824
permission to list, change, or administer
all secrets.

13
00:00:40.824 --> 00:00:43.955
The store protects custody; the
application remains responsible

14
00:00:43.955 --> 00:00:46.802
for its use of the returned value.

15
00:00:46.802 --> 00:00:50.692
The bootstrap issue is called secret zero:
the application needs

16
00:00:50.692 --> 00:00:55.056
a way to authenticate to the store
containing its other credentials.

17
00:00:55.056 --> 00:00:59.041
Installing a permanent master password
merely moves the original problem.

18
00:00:59.041 --> 00:01:03.026
Where supported, use workload identity to
authenticate to the store,

19
00:01:03.026 --> 00:01:07.011
then authorize that identity to retrieve
only the required secret.

20
00:01:07.011 --> 00:01:10.617
The example shows this architecture
through conceptual client APIs.

21
00:01:10.617 --> 00:01:12.894
Retrieve secrets with an authenticated
server-side

22
00:01:12.894 --> 00:01:15.740
SDK or a controlled runtime injection
mechanism.

23
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.536
Use a narrow application accessor with a
fixed secret identifier

24
00:01:19.536 --> 00:01:23.900
rather than letting an incoming HTTP
request choose arbitrary secret names.

25
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.988
Validate availability without printing
values.

26
00:01:25.988 --> 00:01:29.878
A TypeScript type alias named Secret
provides no runtime confidentiality,

27
00:01:29.878 --> 00:01:34.242
and a non-null assertion does not validate
a missing environment value.

28
00:01:34.242 --> 00:01:37.848
Fetching on every request adds latency and
dependency load.

29
00:01:37.848 --> 00:01:40.599
Bounded caching can be appropriate, with
refresh

30
00:01:40.599 --> 00:01:43.446
intervals chosen for rotation and
revocation requirements.

31
00:01:43.446 --> 00:01:45.723
Coordinate concurrent refreshes and avoid
serving

32
00:01:45.723 --> 00:01:48.569
a cached value beyond the allowed policy.

33
00:01:48.569 --> 00:01:51.416
Keep material in memory only as needed.

34
00:01:51.416 --> 00:01:54.072
Write a credential file only when the

35
00:01:54.072 --> 00:01:56.919
consuming system requires it, with
restrictive permissions,

36
00:01:56.919 --> 00:01:59.860
controlled storage, and a defined removal
lifecycle.

